  "title": "Erdős Problem #600",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "markdown-latex",
    "text": "Let $e(n,r)$ be minimal such that every graph on $n$ vertices with at least $e(n,r)$ edges, each edge contained in at least one triangle, must have an edge contained in at least $r$ triangles. Let $r\\geq 2$. Is it true that $ e(n,r+1)-e(n,r)\\to \\infty $ as $n\\to \\infty$? Is it true that $ \\frac{e(n,r+1)}{e(n,r)}\\to 1 $ as $n\\to \\infty$?",
